  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="e6536b2f-2f70-568c-9dfd-342d6bfb512d" activityNameId="2cc609cb-b3bd-5302-96f2-bc17be2cdcb4" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Airport</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Airport
The data represents the airport in Zurich (uniqueairport) and is based on assumptions as follows: \nFoundation layer: 40 cm gravel and a life span of 100 years.\nConcrete floor: 22 cm with a concrete reinforcement of 1.8 kg steel/m2. and a lifespan of 30 years  .\nTransport distance 25 km.\nFor material and energy consumption due to the construction and disposal of buildings we employed generic modules as available from [Kellenberger, 2003 #94]. In order to facilitate a first estimate we made the assumptions as follows:\n· 70% of the built up area is occupied with building halls \n· 30% are multi storey buildings with an average of 5 floors. The assumed height between the floors is 2 .7m\n\n;
UUID: a86b716d-de3e-3d0d-8813-d4d53a8774be
The inventory includes material consumption and energy expenditures related with the construction sealed area (aircraft parking, runways, etc...) at airports. In addition, expenditures for buildings are considered. The latter include expenditures due to refurbishment and demolition.
Location: Data refers the conditions at the major airport in Switzerland.
Technology: Data represents current technologies
Time period: The data  represents the condition in the early 1990’ties. However, the data presented is adjusted by a factor of 1.5 to represent current conditions.
Production volume: na
Sampling: literature studies and oral communication. 
Extrapolations: &lt;null&gt;
Uncertainty adjustments: Data from one airport in Switzerland is used as an estimate for Europe
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_a86b716d-de3e-3d0d-8813-d4d53a8774be.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
